{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And He made for them.</b> In many places we find a masculine form applied to a feminine. 'And Elohim delivered the flock of your father and gave it to me' (Genesis 31:9); 'and the shepherds came and drove them away' (Exodus 2:17). And many more." "<b>Houses.</b> These are sons, for a person's sons are called 'house' — *mesnie* in the vernacular. As we find with David: 'and he tells you that a house shall be made for you' etc. (2 Samuel 7:11); 'and I will raise up your seed after you' etc. (2 Samuel 7:12); 'and I will establish his kingdom' (2 Samuel 7:12). So too here, He gave them sons, and goes on to explain how. Another interpretation: Pharaoh made them houses, to stand in them so that they (the Hebrew women) would not budge from them, and he ordered that the Hebrew women be brought there at the time of childbirth, to cast the males into the river."], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "The vernacular term 'mesnie' (מישנד״א) is Old French, meaning 'household' or 'retinue,' matching the gloss that 'sons' are called 'house.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:55:49.462747380Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}